# Force LF line endings for files that are consumed by Linux tooling.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# On Windows, core.autocrlf=true (the common default) rewrites text files
# to CRLF on checkout and stores them as LF. That is fine for Python, but
# FATAL for shell scripts: a CRLF heredoc terminator (EOF\r) never matches,
# so `sh deploy/generate-selfsigned.sh` fails with "unexpected end of file"
# or writes a corrupt config. The Dockerfile's embedded /bin/sh entrypoint
# and docker-compose.yml commands have the same exposure.
#
# `eol=lf` pins these paths so a Windows checkout still produces
# container-safe files. -text on the certificate paths prevents Git from
# attempting any normalisation on binary key material.
#
# Note: any *existing* CRLF working copy is only converted once
# `git add --renormalize .` is run (see the commit note in deploy/README.md).

# Shell scripts — heredocs and quoting are CRLF-sensitive.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf

# Container definitions — embedded shell run by /bin/sh in Linux images.
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf
docker-compose*.yml  text eol=lf

# Never touch key material or certificates.
*.pem           -text
*.key           -text
*.crt           -text

# Keep the existing behaviour for the rest of the repository unchanged:
# let Git use the platform default for the remaining text files.
